  "rationale": "The subject's own obligations are that coverage documentation is rendered from source annotations and stored review results. It must do this without (a) reading saved execution measurements, (b) evaluating exception acceptance, or (c) auditing review freshness. The cited `requirements.index.render_only` is context only, so I did not reassess it.\n\n**Rendering from annotations and stored reviews**\n- `src/execution_coverage/book.rs#mod[tests]/fn[overview_shows_missing_unreadable_and_recorded_reviews_with_current_justifications]` renders the overview from a source `///` justification plus a `{cite}` annotation.\n- It covers each stored review state: missing, true, false, FIXME, invalid JSON and unreadable (the review path is a directory). For each state it asserts the matching \"Recorded review: <strong>…</strong>\" label, the saved rationale or FIXME text, and the diagnostics \"invalid JSON\" and \"cannot read evidence\".\n- It asserts the \"1 documented source items\" count.\n- It changes the source text and justification and asserts the new text is shown.\n\n**No reading of saved execution measurements**\n- `book_ignores_saved_measurements_and_preserves_artifacts` renders once with no captured data. It then records an attempt with a saved test name and line hits and renders again.\n- It asserts the chapters are identical to the first render. It also asserts the saved test name never appears, and that there are no `<select` elements, `-source` paths or measurement-style text such as \"Line coverage:\", \"Captured source\", \"Test runs and provenance\" or \"0 unresolved gaps\".\n- It overwrites `attempt.json` with invalid content and with `{}`. Rendering still succeeds with identical output. The attempt files are preserved, and no `selection-cache.json` is created. This shows the saved measurement metadata is not consulted.\n- `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[book_rendering_preserves_retained_coverage_and_never_launches_tests]` runs the real `habeas mdbook` binary against a fixture with real captured coverage.\n  - A `cargo` shim records every call and fails any call other than `metadata`.\n  - The test asserts there are no forbidden calls, the test sentinel is not executed, and retained coverage files are unchanged.\n  - The output is the \"No documented coverage gaps are present.\" chapter, even though `coverage-check` on the same data reports unmeasured functions. So the measurements do not influence the rendered book.\n\n**No evaluation of exception acceptance**\n- Review records with determination true, false or FIXME are all shown only as \"Recorded …\" labels. The test asserts there is no \"current and accepted\" text.\n- In the book test, the omitted-text list rejects \"CI would\" and \"0 unresolved gaps\". The overview text states that `habeas coverage-check` is what enforces coverage. Together these show the renderer does not judge whether an exception is accepted.\n\n**No auditing of review freshness**\n- The stored review has `\"hash\": \"old hash\"` and empty `inputs`. It is still displayed as \"Recorded true\", and the test asserts there is no \"input is stale\" text.\n- The test then rewrites the source twice, once with changed code and once with the justification removed. The saved record is still displayed, and the file bytes are unchanged (`assert_eq!(fs::read(&review), before)`). It asserts `target` is never created.\n\nI found no untested obligation or weak assertion. All three tests carry `{test}` references to the subject. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
